**Color-of-Law Misconduct vs. Federal Agency Attribution:** When evaluating official misconduct under **42 U.S.C. § 1983**, liability attaches to state or local officials who misuse state authority to deprive individuals of rights protected by the Constitution or federal law (e.g., Fourth Amendment unlawful seizure or Fourteenth Amendment due process violations).

However, claims implicating federal agencies such as the FBI, DHS, or CIA require a distinct evidentiary threshold. Section 1983 does not apply directly to federal action. Attribution of misconduct to a federal agency requires concrete documentary evidence establishing: \text{Agency} \longrightarrow \text{Individual Officer} \longrightarrow \text{Date/Timestamp} \longrightarrow \text{Specific Action} \longrightarrow \text{Claimed Authority} \longrightarrow \text{Corroborating Agency Record} Vague assertions of federal involvement, broad agency references, or unverified operational codenames do not constitute legal proof of federal participation.

